diagnosis_text:
No fault found. The web service for web1.local is up, reachable, and serving content. From the user’s machine (host_1_1_1_1), DNS resolves web1.local to 10.200.0.4 via the site DNS (nameserver 10.200.0.2), ping succeeds with low RTT (~1.3 ms), and HTTP requests return 200 OK rapidly (curl total ~5 ms) with the expected page (“Welcome to Web Server 1”). Cross-checking from another client (host_2_1_1_1) shows the same healthy behavior.

On the server side (web_server_1), Apache is running and listening on 0.0.0.0:80 (ss shows apache2 pid=58 on port 80; netstat confirms LISTEN). Processes are present (root/apache2 and workers), and clients reliably fetch content. One confusing artifact: systemctl reports apache2 as “inactive (dead)”, which doesn’t match reality. That’s due to the lab’s containerized environment where systemd isn’t the active init for the running process; the web server was started outside systemd, so systemctl’s state is not authoritative here.

Root cause: No current outage—service, DNS, and routing are all healthy; the reported symptom is not reproducible. Corrective fix: No action required. As a hardening measure, consider managing Apache under systemd (or a supervisor) with Restart=on-failure and ensure it’s enabled, plus add simple HTTP health checks/monitoring to detect any future transient issues. === END ===